{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c3d1","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0c8e2-bf86-7413-8196-b3968750220e"},"sequence":67,"recorded_at":1780531400000129,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"b7feb475-a410-4d2f-87a4-514d078fdaaf","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"b7feb475-a410-4d2f-87a4-514d078fdaaf","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"b7feb475-a410-4d2f-87a4-514d078fdaaf"},"call_id":"call_01a0c8e2df9c7042908e9156f05c2c29","text":"Read text file `/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.temp/resources/TASK-260830-1c28dz/TASK-260830-1c28dz_rework-rev7.md`.\n1|THIS BRIEF SUPERSEDES TASK-260830-1c28dz_rework-rev6.md and every earlier precondition. Do exactly what is written here.\n2|Rework run for TASK-260830-1c28dz. Revision 6 was reviewed by RUN-260922-792301 (codex gpt-6-astra low) and routed CHANGES REQUESTED: **one P1**, two P2, one P3. Read TASK-260830-1c28dz_review-verdict-rev6.md first.\n3|**The structural work landed.** The closure-authority invariant that failed at three successive boundaries is closed — the reviewer found no fourth hole in it — and the closure recheck with its mutex closes the quiescence interleaving from revision 5. The identity binding, the refresh bound, and the importer outcome corpus all hold. Five P1 in revision 1, four, three, two, two, and now one: keep every one of those closures.\n4|\n5|1. **The single P1 and P2-A are the same invariant, and naming it is the whole job of this revision.** The reviewer labelled the P1 `repeat-of: revision-5 P1-B's admission-to-effect interval, now at generation rather than closure`. You fixed the interval for **closure**; the interval itself is still open for every other authorization fact.\n6|   - **P1 — generation admission goes stale while attach waits for its new lock.** `ops.go:102-107` obtains and checks server attestation **before** `barrierMu.Lock()` at line 116; after acquiring the lock it rechecks only closure and then creates the durable writable-client receipt at line 127. There is no generation recheck at the effect boundary. `TestReviewAttachGenerationAfterLockWait` holds the lifecycle barrier lock, starts `Execute(attach)`, waits for a valid old-generation admission, changes the live generation source to `generation-two` before releasing the lock — and attach succeeds with `InputAuthorized:true` under the old request generation. Fails twice under `-race`, no data-race diagnostic, no production mutation. SPEC §4.C requires generation and authorization to be rechecked immediately before each side effect. B42's cross-process and already-returned-vector bounds do not cover a receipt admitted under a generation that changed before this same Lifecycle acquired its lock.\n7|   - **P2-A — attach commits a new receipt after its operation deadline.** `ops.go:81` checks the deadline only before the potentially blocking `ServerAdmission` and lock acquisition; neither the post-lock block nor `backend.createAttachClient` rechecks the request deadline, and the latter's authorization-expiry recheck is a **different timestamp**. With the clock at 12:00, a request deadline of 18:00 and authorization valid until the next day, a `ServerAdmission` that advances the clock to 19:00 still yields success, a previously absent durable receipt, and writable argv without `-r`. Row 85's claim that deadlines are checked before every effect is false for attach.\n8|   **So deliver the enumeration, not two more rechecks.** List every authorization fact the attach path carries — closure, server generation, request deadline, authorization expiry, transport class, input-binding, and anything else in those structures — and for each state: where it is first observed, every wait or lock acquisition that can occur between that observation and the effect, and whether it is revalidated at the effect boundary. Then make the effect boundary revalidate all of them by construction, keeping the request deadline distinct from authorization expiry, and include the lock-wait in the deadline contract. Add a regression whose **operation expires while its authorization remains valid**. If the same interval exists on the create, restore or stop paths, say so and either close it there too or state it as a bound with its own row — a reviewer will look, because this is the third round in which an interval was closed for one fact and left open for the next.\n9|2. **P2-B — the false-to-true input escalation has no effect-level negative witness.** Reviewer narrowing A3 changes only the landed `CheckAttachRequest` input-binding clause so that it still rejects a false request against true authorization but **admits a true request against false authorization** — one site, used by both the lifecycle entry and the durable attach store. The committed `tmuxserver` suite and the `terminalbackend`/`termbind` owner suites each survive it twice. Under the mutant `Execute` does eventually return `terminal_backend_unauthorized` from `CheckAttachResult`, but **the durable writable receipt has already been committed**. So an assertion about the returned error is not enough: commit the composed negative test and its narrowing row asserting the **absence of the receipt** as well as the refusal, and do not fork the landed authorization gate. (The reviewer's broader `go test ./... -count=2` attempt exited 1 on pre-existing `environ` refusal-site audit failures — which reproduce on the unchanged candidate — and a `sessquery` timeout; those are explicitly not credited as kills, and the three relevant packages passed both repetitions. Do not treat that exit as a signal about your change.)\n10|3. **P3 — two of your reported narrowing rows are positive-path wiring mutations.** Read the verdict's two items, replace them with real narrowings of the gate they claim to measure, and correct the row text.\n11|\n12|PROCESS. Keep the candidate UNCOMMITTED; exactly one leaf's worth of delta on top of the checkpoint; base `d4bd91d0`, and if trunk moves refresh through the managed path and repeat the path-by-path audit. Never commit on the Story branch, never edit `.task-board` directly, never self-accept, and do NOT touch `internal/traceability` — the final leaf g0pcnt carries the registry. Keep the zero-real-tmux determinism property. Run your own package tests, the full importer closure, `GOOS=windows GOARCH=amd64 go vet ./...`, and the harness BEFORE handoff, then the full configured suite once from `spawn.worktree_isolation.validation.commands`. Update the results with a finding-by-finding table and the effect-boundary fact enumeration from item 1; update the conformance matrix and the evidence archive, attach the results standalone, verify every attachment by reading it back off the board, then `task-board handoff TASK-260830-1c28dz --role developer` so revision 7 is constructed, and exit promptly. Hand off no later than 3 hours in. Canonical CLI /Users/iv/.curator/global/bin/task-board. Model: muse-spark max. The independent reviewer will be codex gpt-6-astra low.\n13|REJECTION PATTERNS THE INDEPENDENT REVIEWER HAS ALREADY CAUGHT ON THIS EPIC (2026-09-17: TASK-260830-1geqhj rev1, TASK-260830-24z2b3 rev2, TASK-260830-219okr rev3 were all routed CHANGES REQUESTED, and between them these six shapes account for every finding). Read this as a checklist you run against your own work BEFORE handoff; each item names what a reviewer will do to your candidate.\n14|(a) Circular expectations. An assertion that compares a production value against a production constant (`got.Code == pkg.SomeUnsupportedCode`) pins nothing: swapping the constant to another registered value with the same exit keeps the suite green. Assert the LITERAL token from the pinned specification text, at the production entry, and make sure the literal actually occurs in a committed test — not only in a comment. Derive every expectation from the spec, never from the implementation.\n15|(b) A rule pinned at the helper instead of the entry. A test that calls the inner helper directly proves the helper; it says nothing about whether the composed production entry still reaches it. Drive every rule through the entry point the conformance matrix names as its call site, and if a rule is implemented TWICE (a Build path and a Decode path, an exported entry and its internal sibling), each side needs its own narrowing mutant — a rule pinned only on one side leaves the other side unmeasured, and reviewers plant exactly there.\n16|(c) Forking a landed gate. Never re-implement what internal/environ, internal/canonicaljson, internal/scalar or another landed owner already gates (string bounds in CHARACTERS not bytes, uint53, digests, UUIDv7, timestamps, sorted-unique arrays, closed-shape decoding, the AX number model that forbids floating point, values at or beyond 2^53 and rounding-and-continuing). Delegate to it and keep only your package-specific rules. Every fork this epic produced became a defect: a byte-measured bound, an extension value rounded at 2^60, nested duplicate members collapsed by a lenient decode.\n17|(d) A census or an inventory standing in for behaviour. A reflection census of field NAMES does not prove that a cached observation cannot authorize; an arm inventory keyed by message text does not prove a condition edit is caught. Drive the input, assert the effect.\n18|(e) Mutant rows that do not measure what they claim. An arm-delete (`&& false`) is not a narrowing; a row patching two sites whose kill comes from one is one measured site reported as two; a plant with no raw log and no subprocess exit is a verdict line, not evidence. Ship one raw log per plant per run, state narrowing vs arm-delete per row, and include an applied harmless SURVIVED control so the harness is proven able to report a survivor.\n19|(f) Prose that outruns the tests. \"Composes X\", \"never admits what decoding refuses\", \"nothing re-implemented\", \"no cached sentinel can authorize\" are censusable claims: a reviewer greps for the callers and plants the counter-example. Before you write a sentence like that in a doc comment, README, LOGBOOK or TRACEABILITY row, make a test fail without the property. If you cannot, state it as an explicit BOUND instead, naming what is not covered.\n20|Finally: report the coverage ratio you MEASURED, not the one you planned. Two of the three rejected revisions claimed a complete ratio and the reviewer measured a smaller one; an honest smaller number with named gaps is accepted, an inflated one is a finding.","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c3d3","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0c8e2-bf86-7413-8196-b3968750220e"},"sequence":68,"recorded_at":1780531400000131,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"b7feb475-a410-4d2f-87a4-514d078fdaaf","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"b7feb475-a410-4d2f-87a4-514d078fdaaf","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"b7feb475-a410-4d2f-87a4-514d078fdaaf"},"call_id":"call_01a0c8e2e1917573884f8f7e19549a00","text":"Read text file `/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.temp/resources/TASK-260830-1c28dz/TASK-260830-1c28dz_reviewer-cr6.md`.\n1|Independently review Change Request revision 6 of TASK-260830-1c28dz (implement-tmux-lifecycle-operations), the SECOND of three leaves of STORY-260830-2t4g7i (production-tmux-backend). The chain is 35urbp -> 1c28dz -> g0pcnt; 35urbp (private tmux server management) is the signed checkpoint `1ba06e9` on the Story branch and g0pcnt carries the registry. Change Request shape: task_delta on the Story branch (trunk base 40bb8c9; checkpointed predecessors d4bd91d ; tip d4bd91d0e740285b21b8d65bf6f074c8009b04ae), published by RUN-260922-6f89bb on muse-spark max, 44 changed paths, patch and validation log attached as TASK-260830-1c28dz_change-request_rev6.patch / _rev6-validation.log. Revisions 1..5 are earlier construction attempts of the same producer scope that failed the Change Request validation suite on the pre-PR51 10m race-test timeout under concurrent suites; they carry no review verdict and no prior review findings — review revision 6 on its own merits.. Read the producer brief TASK-260830-1c28dz_producer.md, the outcome TASK-260830-1c28dz_results.md, TASK-260830-1c28dz_conformance-matrix.md, TASK-260830-1c28dz_producer-evidence.tar.gz, the predecessor's accepted evidence (TASK-260830-35urbp_results.md and its verdict TASK-260830-35urbp_review-verdict-rev7.md), the CR patch and validation log, and the exact immutable CR bytes.\n2|\n3|NORMATIVE AUTHORITY: the pinned `internal/specdoc/SPEC.v0.7.0.md` **§4.2** (1403-1449, including the five-step after-restore sequence at 1433-1441) and **§3.2** (806-813: the runtime root, the socket path `<runtime>/tmux/ax.sock`, and component-by-component custody without following a symlink), with **§4.C** (1082), **§4.D** (1220), **§4.E** (1309) and **§4.4** (1478-1490) as context. The task record still names v0.5.0 and that is stale — a candidate citing a v0.5.0 line number or quoting text absent from the pinned file is a finding. Derive every expectation from the spec text, never from a production constant.\n4|\n5|REVISION HISTORY: **every** earlier revision of this leaf was routed CHANGES REQUESTED, and their verdicts are attached as `TASK-260830-1c28dz_review-verdict-rev<N>.md` for every N below the revision you are reviewing. No sentence in this brief asserts that any revision was clean — if you find one, it is a template defect, so trust the attached verdicts over this text and say so in your own verdict. Read the verdict for the revision immediately before yours, and the rework brief that answered it, before grading anything as closed or recurring; use `repeat-of:` when a finding is the same class at an adjacent site.\n6|\n7|Verify with your own instruments, on isolated immutable copies of the exact candidate tree; never mutate the live Story worktree, index, branch or HEAD; no product edits, commits, checkpoints or integration. Another Story is producing concurrently on this host, so record wall-clock and load for any gate you time.\n8|\n9|(1) **Eight operations, eight witnesses.** Create, attach, status, quiesce, safe-boundary, stop, stale termination, restore. Check each is driven through a production entry by a named committed test and that the results state the n-of-8 ratio with a call site per operation. One operation's test standing in for another's is a finding; so is an operation reported as driven whose test reaches only a helper.\n10|\n11|(2) **The after-restore sequence is ordered, and its order is the property.** §4.2 1433-1441: read the latest locally known lease; attempt a mesh lease refresh without blocking forever; resume locally ONLY under a winning local lease with valid materialization; offer remote attach/takeover when another host owns the session in an interactive terminal; otherwise enter `parked` WITHOUT launching the provider. Drive each branch and assert the outcome. Plant a reordering — an earlier step's refusal swallowing a later step's offer is exactly the defect a sibling Story shipped and had to repair, so check that the offer branch is reachable under a lapsed local grant, and that \"without blocking forever\" has a measured bound rather than a sentence.\n12|\n13|(3) **The four handovers from the first leaf.** That leaf shipped the decision core and argv only and named this leaf as owner for: the exec and probe adapters (its `Dependencies` had NO production implementation — verify they exist now and are driven, and that the server socket carries crash and idempotency evidence or an explicit purity bound); **B18** the `sun_path` limit (104 bytes on macOS) against the derived socket path; **B16** an `ax` invocation from inside its own AX pane refusing `ambient collision`; and **B19/B20** the SPEC 810-812 before-bind rejection of a socket path, parent or ancestor with unsafe ownership or permissions — a 0777 root is admitted today on every first-leaf entry, so check whether this leaf's bind step rejects it or states why not. Grade each as closed with its test, deferred with reason and owner, or silently dropped.\n14|\n15|(4) **The census is a deliverable, and its asymmetry is where to attack.** Verify the gate × entry census exists, that its counts reproduce mechanically, and that its per-entry kill attribution holds for the cells marked Measured. Then pick a gate it marks Measured at one entry and plant a narrowing at a DIFFERENT entry that reaches the same gate. Check the two-sided-gate symmetry lines: the predecessor's last P2 was a gate whose two sides carried different row counts, and its own census displayed the asymmetry without flagging it. A cell reading \"pinned at the helper\" and nothing else is the hole that cost that leaf six rounds.\n16|\n17|(5) **Determinism, verified not read.** Every rule must be driven to its effect by a committed test that passes with NO tmux process anywhere; any real-tmux test must be separately gated and never the only witness for an acceptance row. Verify by running the suite in an environment where the tmux binary cannot be resolved, and report exactly which tests fail or skip — a row whose only witness skips there is unmeasured however green the producer's log looks. Check the converse too: a rule degraded into a mock-only shape so it could be tested is a finding, not a solution; the production entry must still be the thing under test.\n18|\n19|(6) **No forked owners, and composition proven by driving.** `internal/tmuxserver` (the predecessor), `internal/terminalbackend`, `internal/axpane`, `internal/terminstance`, `internal/termbind`, `internal/secprim`, `internal/localstore`, `internal/fencing`, `internal/sessstate` are landed. Grep for a second model of anything they own. The results must name which existing type or gate was extended and which deliberately was not — verify that claim rather than accepting it. Then take outcome grids (not name-keyed per-test lists) over the complete importer set on the CR base and on the candidate and diff them; every class that moved must be named in the results with its before and after. If a landed test was changed to accept new behaviour, grade the argument and its spec citation — a landed regression test rewritten without one is a P1.\n20|\n21|(7) **Traceability boundary.** This leaf must NOT touch `internal/traceability` (registry, digest pin, README measured-coverage subsection) — that belongs to g0pcnt. Any edit there is a finding. A package-local TRACEABILITY.md and the conformance matrix are the right places.\n22|\n23|(8) **Mutation and hygiene.** Rerun the shipped harness in an isolated copy, rerun each KILLED twice, and plant at least four narrowings of your own on gates the producer did not mutate, plus an applied harmless SURVIVED control; per-plant raw logs, subprocess exits, precise denominators, NOT_APPLIED is not a pass. `PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1`, zero `__pycache__`/`.pyc` in the CR tree or your copies. gofmt/vet clean including `GOOS=windows GOARCH=amd64 go vet ./...` — the predecessor lost a round to a test file that reassigned a unix-only seam with no build constraint, and the local suite's `GOOS=windows go build` compiles no test files, so construction being green proves nothing there. The candidate byte-identical to trunk outside this Story's scope: check every path in `git diff --name-only <the CR base> <current origin/main>` that also appears in the candidate, because a refresh carries stale pre-refresh copies that read as reverts, and `task-board.config.json` must equal the base byte-for-byte. The full configured suite green on the exact tree; read the command count from the candidate config.\n24|\n25|Report the coverage ratio YOU measured against the acceptance table's real row count, not the producer's restated one. Attach TASK-260830-1c28dz_review-verdict-rev6.md and TASK-260830-1c28dz_review-evidence-rev6.tar.gz (a real gzip archive), complete the live merged checklist, then `accept_cr(TASK-260830-1c28dz, revision=6, evidence=TASK-260830-1c28dz_review-verdict-rev6.md)` or hand off changes requested with P1/P2/P3 classes named and a short \"Rework scope (for the producer)\" section at the end. Model: gpt-6-astra low. Canonical CLI /Users/iv/.curator/global/bin/task-board.","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c3d5","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0c8e2-bf86-7413-8196-b3968750220e"},"sequence":69,"recorded_at":1780531400000133,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"b7feb475-a410-4d2f-87a4-514d078fdaaf","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"b7feb475-a410-4d2f-87a4-514d078fdaaf","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"b7feb475-a410-4d2f-87a4-514d078fdaaf"},"call_id":"call_01a0c8e2e38772808eab1825fc2ff1fe","text":"Read text file `/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.temp/resources/TASK-260830-1c28dz/TASK-260830-1c28dz_producer.md`.\n1|Implement TASK-260830-1c28dz (implement-tmux-lifecycle-operations), the SECOND of three leaves of STORY-260830-2t4g7i (production-tmux-backend). The first leaf TASK-260830-35urbp (private tmux server management) was accepted at revision 7 and is the signed checkpoint `1ba06e9` on the Story branch; the final leaf TASK-260830-g0pcnt (generation and attach semantics) follows you and carries the traceability registry. This is the largest leaf of the Story.\n2|\n3|AUTHORITY. The task record's Scope names the spec at v0.5.0. That reference is STALE: this repository adopted v0.7.0 and the pinned copy is `internal/specdoc/SPEC.v0.7.0.md`. Your authority is **§4.2 tmux backend** (lines 1403-1449) — including the five-step after-restore sequence at 1433-1441 — **§3.2** (806-813, which fixes the runtime root and the socket path `<runtime>/tmux/ax.sock` and its component-by-component custody), with **§4.C Lifecycle and operations** (1082), **§4.D Closed capabilities and evidence** (1220), **§4.E** (1309) and **§4.4** (1478-1490) as context. §3.2 was missing from the first leaf's brief and cost it a round when the reviewer found the candidate diverging; it is named here so that does not repeat. Quote the pinned file, never the network, never a v0.5.0 line number.\n4|\n5|WHAT THIS LEAF MUST DELIVER. Create, attach, status, quiesce, safe-boundary, stop, stale termination and restore, through structured commands. Eight operations: report them as an explicit n-of-8 ratio with the production call site for each, and never let one operation's test stand in for another's.\n6|\n7|**READ THE FIRST LEAF BEFORE YOU WRITE A LINE.** `git -C .temp/STORY-260830-2t4g7i/worktree show --stat HEAD`, then `TASK-260830-35urbp_results.md`, its conformance matrix and `TASK-260830-35urbp_review-verdict-rev7.md`. That leaf shipped the decision core and argv only and **explicitly declared as YOUR bound** the things it did not build. Four handovers are waiting for you and each must be dispositioned in your results:\n8|- **The exec and probe adapters do not exist.** `Dependencies` in the first leaf has no production implementation anywhere in the tree — nothing execs a server. Building them is this leaf's job, and with them comes the server socket's crash and idempotency evidence, or an explicit purity bound.\n9|- **B18: the derived socket path is not checked against the platform `sun_path` limit** (104 bytes on macOS). A deep state root surfaces as a spawn failure in YOUR adapter. Decide it deliberately.\n10|- **B16: an `ax` invocation from inside its own AX pane** (`TMUX=<derived>,<pid>,<idx>`) refuses `ambient collision` when the caller populates `Ambient` from the process environment. Over-strict rather than over-permissive, and the caller owns what it observes — but the first leaf's reviewer explicitly left the deliberate decision to you.\n11|- **B19/B20: SPEC 810-812 requires rejecting a socket path, parent or ancestor whose ownership or permissions are unsafe BEFORE bind or connect.** The first leaf states the `localstore` layout owner for root creation custody and names YOUR bind step for the before-bind rejection. A 0777 root is admitted today on every first-leaf entry.\n12|\n13|**BUILD THE CENSUS FROM THE START. THIS IS THE SINGLE MOST EXPENSIVE LESSON OF THIS STORY.** The first leaf took SEVEN revisions, and every round after the second had the same shape: a rule witnessed at ONE of the several production entries that reach it, so a narrowing at any other entry walked through a green suite. The fix was not more tests, it was the instrument. Ship a **gate × entry census** as its own table in the conformance matrix, mirrored in TRACEABILITY: for every gate, list every production entry that can reach it, and give each cell exactly one of — a named committed test driving the gate THROUGH that entry plus an admitting narrowing row it kills; `unreachable` with the reason; or a stated BOUND naming what is unmeasured and who owns it. \"Pinned at the helper\" is not a cell: a helper pin plus an entry that reaches the same gate is two cells. For every gate implemented on two or more sides, compare the row sets side by side and report a symmetry line — the first leaf's last P2 was exactly a gate whose two sides had different row counts, and its own census showed the asymmetry without flagging it. Report the measured cell ratio.\n14|\n15|DETERMINISM IS A HARD CONSTRAINT AND IT SHAPES THE DESIGN. AGENTS.md requires tests that are deterministic and free of network or terminal dependencies. tmux 3.6a is installed on this host; that is NOT a licence to depend on it. Every rule must be driven to its effect by a committed test that passes with no tmux process anywhere. If you also write a test that talks to a real tmux server it must be separately gated and must NOT be the only witness for any acceptance row — state in the results which rows have a real-tmux witness in addition to their deterministic one. How you achieve that is your design decision; do not degrade a rule into a mock-only shape to make it testable, and do not ask. If a rule genuinely cannot be driven without a live server, say so as an explicit BOUND with the reason.\n16|\n17|THE LANDED-CODE HAZARD. You are composing with landed packages (`internal/terminalbackend`, `internal/axpane`, `internal/terminstance`, `internal/termbind`, `internal/tmuxserver` from your predecessor, `internal/secprim`, `internal/localstore`, `internal/fencing`, `internal/sessstate`). Do not fork any of them — read them first and say in the results which existing type or gate you extended and which you deliberately did not. Every fork this epic produced became a defect. And prove composition by driving it: compare **outcomes**, not test names, over the complete importer set on the base and on your candidate, and name every class that moved with its before and after. A name-keyed per-test diff scoped to one package reported \"only additions\" on a sibling Story while eight rows elsewhere changed meaning under unchanged names — that is how a regression reached review.\n18|\n19|TRACEABILITY. Do NOT edit `internal/traceability` (ownership registry, digest pin, README measured-coverage subsection) — the final leaf g0pcnt carries the registry bindings and the re-pin. Document clause coverage in a package-local TRACEABILITY.md and in the conformance matrix.\n20|\n21|EVIDENCE CONTRACT (repository DoD). Every AC row driven through a production entry by a named committed test with a stated n-of-m ratio and call sites; a negative for every refusal, asserting the literal spec token rather than a production constant; a narrowing mutant per gate executed by a shipped harness with per-plant raw logs, subprocess exits and an applied harmless SURVIVED control (`PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1`, no `__pycache__` anywhere); crash and idempotency evidence for every durable write or an explicit purity bound; a README package section with test commands and NO capability or CLI claim; a LOGBOOK entry newest-first.\n22|\n23|WORKSPACE. The managed Story worktree `.temp/STORY-260830-2t4g7i/worktree` ALREADY EXISTS on branch `task-board/story/STORY-260830-2t4g7i` and its tip is your predecessor's signed checkpoint `1ba06e9` — do not re-provision it, do not rebase it, do not commit on it, do not amend or revert the checkpoint, never edit `.task-board` directly and never self-accept. Your candidate is the UNCOMMITTED working tree on top of that checkpoint: exactly one leaf's worth of delta. If origin/main moves before your handoff run `task-board worktree refresh-candidate TASK-260830-1c28dz`, then audit every path in `git diff --name-only <old-base> <new-base>` that also appears in your `git status` and restore trunk content by hand — a refresh carries stale pre-refresh copies of trunk-changed files into the candidate and they read as reverts; `task-board.config.json` in particular must equal the new base byte-for-byte. Before handoff `git status` shows only candidate paths.\n24|\n25|PROCESS. Read the Curator-managed `go-testing-tools` skill first. All CI is local; never trigger hosted CI. Run your own package tests, the importer set, `GOOS=windows GOARCH=amd64 go vet ./...` (the first leaf lost a round to a test file that reassigned a unix-only seam with no build constraint — the local suite runs `GOOS=windows go build`, which compiles no test files, so a green construction proves nothing about this), and the harness BEFORE handoff, then the full configured suite once, reading the commands from `spawn.worktree_isolation.validation.commands` in the candidate config. Command 5 is the race gate with `-timeout 25m`; another Story is producing concurrently on this host, so expect it to be slow and do NOT shorten it — if a gate times out, report the measured elapsed time and host load rather than arguing it away. **Resolve every artifact path ABSOLUTELY and verify what you attached by reading it back off the board**: the control root and the Story worktree both have a `.temp/TASK-260830-1c28dz/`, and a sibling Story lost a whole round by attaching the worktree's stale copies. Report the coverage ratio you MEASURED. Hand off no later than 3.5 hours in; if the full scope cannot be finished, hand off a reviewable slice with the remaining items enumerated precisely in the results. Attach TASK-260830-1c28dz_results.md, TASK-260830-1c28dz_conformance-matrix.md and TASK-260830-1c28dz_producer-evidence.tar.gz (a real gzip archive), keep the checklist current, then `task-board handoff TASK-260830-1c28dz --role developer` and exit promptly. Canonical CLI /Users/iv/.curator/global/bin/task-board. Model: muse-spark max. The independent reviewer will be codex gpt-6-astra low.\n26|REJECTION PATTERNS THE INDEPENDENT REVIEWER HAS ALREADY CAUGHT ON THIS EPIC (2026-09-17: TASK-260830-1geqhj rev1, TASK-260830-24z2b3 rev2, TASK-260830-219okr rev3 were all routed CHANGES REQUESTED, and between them these six shapes account for every finding). Read this as a checklist you run against your own work BEFORE handoff; each item names what a reviewer will do to your candidate.\n27|(a) Circular expectations. An assertion that compares a production value against a production constant (`got.Code == pkg.SomeUnsupportedCode`) pins nothing: swapping the constant to another registered value with the same exit keeps the suite green. Assert the LITERAL token from the pinned specification text, at the production entry, and make sure the literal actually occurs in a committed test — not only in a comment. Derive every expectation from the spec, never from the implementation.\n28|(b) A rule pinned at the helper instead of the entry. A test that calls the inner helper directly proves the helper; it says nothing about whether the composed production entry still reaches it. Drive every rule through the entry point the conformance matrix names as its call site, and if a rule is implemented TWICE (a Build path and a Decode path, an exported entry and its internal sibling), each side needs its own narrowing mutant — a rule pinned only on one side leaves the other side unmeasured, and reviewers plant exactly there.\n29|(c) Forking a landed gate. Never re-implement what internal/environ, internal/canonicaljson, internal/scalar or another landed owner already gates (string bounds in CHARACTERS not bytes, uint53, digests, UUIDv7, timestamps, sorted-unique arrays, closed-shape decoding, the AX number model that forbids floating point, values at or beyond 2^53 and rounding-and-continuing). Delegate to it and keep only your package-specific rules. Every fork this epic produced became a defect: a byte-measured bound, an extension value rounded at 2^60, nested duplicate members collapsed by a lenient decode.\n30|(d) A census or an inventory standing in for behaviour. A reflection census of field NAMES does not prove that a cached observation cannot authorize; an arm inventory keyed by message text does not prove a condition edit is caught. Drive the input, assert the effect.\n31|(e) Mutant rows that do not measure what they claim. An arm-delete (`&& false`) is not a narrowing; a row patching two sites whose kill comes from one is one measured site reported as two; a plant with no raw log and no subprocess exit is a verdict line, not evidence. Ship one raw log per plant per run, state narrowing vs arm-delete per row, and include an applied harmless SURVIVED control so the harness is proven able to report a survivor.\n32|(f) Prose that outruns the tests. \"Composes X\", \"never admits what decoding refuses\", \"nothing re-implemented\", \"no cached sentinel can authorize\" are censusable claims: a reviewer greps for the callers and plants the counter-example. Before you write a sentence like that in a doc comment, README, LOGBOOK or TRACEABILITY row, make a test fail without the property. If you cannot, state it as an explicit BOUND instead, naming what is not covered.\n33|Finally: report the coverage ratio you MEASURED, not the one you planned. Two of the three rejected revisions claimed a complete ratio and the reviewer measured a smaller one; an honest smaller number with named gaps is accepted, an inflated one is a finding.","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
